# Round 17, L §5.8: "A row x[*] = v states every key of x that exists at its version's known (§5.6). A key that a
# later version brings needs its own row, since a version speaks as of its known". A thing entering persons in v2
# (known 2026-06-01) isn't covered by v1's x[*] = 1 (known 2026-01-01): x[jan] has no row, a hole by the gap rule
# (L §4.6); x[ola] is 1. L4_6_closed_world checks the same for an index's key.
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2026-12-31
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source s "s"

kind persons
input x[p in persons]: int

version v1
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2026-01-01
  persons + [ola] lives 1990-01-01..
  x[*] = 1

version v2
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2026-06-01
  persons + [jan] lives 1990-01-01..

ask q1: x[ola], x[jan] law 2026-07-01
=== expect
q1: (1, ?"x at jan: no value at 2026-07-01" (x[jan]))
  law date 2026-07-01, cut 2026-12-31
